from pathlib import Path
import json,html,hashlib,zipfile
P=Path(__file__).parent
r=json.loads((P/'results.json').read_text());c=r['central'];s=r['sensitivities'];b=r['transaction_bridge']
links={'prospectus':'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1999855/000110465926065016/tm2612379d6_424b5.htm','pricing':'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1999855/000110465926064016/tm2612379d9_fwp.htm','performance':'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1999855/000199985526000021/crvna2026-p2servicerrepo.htm','investor':'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1896036/000110465926103176/tm2624152d1_ncsrs.htm','company':'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1690820/000169082026000067/cvna-20260930.htm','ally':'https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1690820/000169082026000055/ex10_9carvana-allyforwardf.htm'}
def link(k,label):return f'<a href="{links[k]}">{label}</a>'
def table(head,rows):return '<table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+str(x)+'</th>' for x in head)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+str(x)+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table>'
body='''<h1>Carvana: can the loan buyer earn an adequate return?</h1><p class="date">Evidence and sensitivity analysis • 30 October 2026 • CRVNA 2026-P2</p>
<p><strong>Verdict: a meaningful securitization premium is economically plausible. We have not validated Carvana’s companywide gain ratio or established subsidized buying.</strong> New public pricing evidence reduces the unidentified portion of one transaction. A monthly cash-flow model shows that the residual buyer can earn a double-digit return under some assumptions, but that return is sensitive to losses, prepayments and the gap between borrower rates and funding coupons.</p>
<p>This is an economic sensitivity model, not a reconstruction of the private buyer’s underwriting case or a valuation opinion. The former CarMax 2.79% companywide benchmark remains withdrawn.</p>
<h2>1. What changed in the evidence</h2>'''
body+=f'''<p>The filed pricing communication marks the transaction “PRICED” and states a $49.272 million value for the entire R class, $46.8084 million offered, and an 18.5% modeled yield using the private certificate memorandum’s Case 2. It also prices N near par. This is stronger evidence than an undisclosed residual assumption, but remains a pricing communication rather than a complete closing settlement. The private Case 2 assumptions and XS sale terms were not recovered. {link('pricing','Filed pricing sheet')}.</p>
<p>A buyer independently reports a P2 residual position: Eagle Point Institutional Income Fund lists $1.431844 million cost, $1.424959 million fair value and a 13.10% estimated effective yield at June 30. That yield is not the pricing sheet’s 18.5% scenario and neither is a realized return. Different modeling, timing or amortized-cost conventions could explain the difference; the disclosures do not resolve it. It must not be silently treated as one agreed hurdle rate. {link('investor','Investor schedule and footnote 6')}.</p>
<h2>2. Transaction structure and consideration</h2>
<p>Carvana LLC / Carvana FAC originate loans → depositor → issuing and grantor trusts → borrower collections allocated among servicing/XS, rated A–D notes, N and residual certificates. Bridgecrest collects payments; ordinary credit losses fall on transaction cash flows and ultimately investors according to priority. Representation breaches and servicing obligations are separate from ordinary default risk. Carvana retains risk interests; public underwriters are not thereby identified as private whole-loan buyers. {link('prospectus','Final prospectus, transaction overview and waterfall')}.</p>'''
body+=table(['Component','USD millions','Evidence / interpretation'],[
 ['Loan principal at May 8 cutoff','1,100.395','Observed; not carrying basis'],
 ['A–D total issue value at public prices',f"{b['AD_all_notes_market_value_m']:,.3f}",'Derived including retained portion; not all cash sold'],
 ['A–D net underwriting cash','1,042.661','Disclosed; after underwriting discount, before other expenses'],
 ['N full-class value at pricing-sheet price','16.500','Filed indicative execution terms; 95% offered'],
 ['R full-class value','49.272','Filed pricing value; 95% offered'],
 ['A–D + N + R total issue value','1,166.062','Derived; excludes XS'],
 ['Initial reserve funding','(8.253)','0.50% main + 0.25% N; do not double-count future releases'],
 ['A–D underwriting discount / estimated other issue expenses','(2.613) / (1.000)','Private placement costs unresolved'],
 ['Partial net issue-value bridge','1,154.196','104.89% of principal; includes retained securities, excludes XS and other missing items'],
 ['XS value at a hypothetical 10% required return',f"{b['modeled_XS_at10pct_m']:,.3f}",'Model only; actual ownership/price unknown'],
 ['Loan carrying value, accrued interest, exact retained FV, transaction gain','Unknown','Cannot turn the bridge into an observed sale gain']])
body+='''<p>The 104.89 figure is a partial issue-value calculation, not cash proceeds of 104.89 cents per dollar or a 4.89% reported gain. Adding modeled XS produces roughly 105.96, still before unresolved costs and basis items. The N reserve’s eventual release belongs to the depositor and is tracked separately from R; its modeled present value is about 0.23 per $100 at a hypothetical 10% discount rate. Whether and how that interest enters the accounting bridge is unresolved. Warehouse repayment is a financing use of proceeds, not additional sale consideration.</p>
<h2>3. Cash-flow model and assumptions</h2>
<p>We use the final prospectus’s 67 illustrative subpools, retaining their balance, rate and term distributions. Their balances reconcile exactly to $1,100,395,400.20. This avoids the unresolved cutoff reconciliation in the earlier loan-level snapshot. As a definition cross-check, all 38,690 initial loan records have an original-amount-weighted contractual rate of 10.539%, close to the disclosed 10.54% APR. It does not establish identity of rate and APR on every loan.</p>
<p>Each month records scheduled principal, interest, defaults, voluntary prepayments, lagged net recoveries, fees and remaining balances. The unlevered valuation discounts those net asset cash flows without subtracting financing costs. The separate waterfall pays the 1% servicing strip, expenses, A–D interest and interleaved parity cures, reserve top-ups and the 0.9% overcollateralization target, then N interest and 65% of remaining cash toward N principal; the residual receives the remainder. XS is separated from the 0.45% base fee. Reserves are funded once and their releases are allocated to the correct owner.</p>'''
body+=table(['Input','Central scenario','Status / alternative'],[
 ['Lifetime net credit loss','3% of initial principal','Assumed; test 2%, 2.75%, 4%, 6%'],
 ['Total monthly ABS attrition speed','1.3%','Pricing convention observed; not a realized forecast or annual CPR'],
 ['Default timing','Shaped hazard peaking at month 18','Assumed; peak months 9 and 30 tested'],
 ['Net recovery / delay','35% / 3 months','Assumed; 20–50% and 6-month delay tested'],
 ['Required whole-loan return','5–9% annual effective','Sensitivity range; no observed Ally hurdle'],
 ['Residual discount / XS discount','18.5% / 10%','R pricing-case input observed; XS hurdle assumed'],
 ['Fees','0.45% base; 0.55% XS; recurring administrative costs','Contract/report inputs; excludes unexpected transition or indemnity costs']])
body+='''<p>ABS speed here includes defaults as part of unscheduled runoff. The model allocates a shaped default hazard within total attrition, then attributes the remaining attrition to voluntary repayments; it does not apply historical all-cause runoff and then add defaults again. Net losses are calibrated as gross defaults less delayed recoveries, not deducted a second time from cash flow.</p>
<p><strong>Limitations:</strong> monthly regular periods replace actual settlement and first-payment stubs; fees are simplified; no optional 2% cleanup call, legal acceleration or investor fund leverage is modeled. No tested scenario has note-interest shortfalls, but this is not a rating stress engine. Exact timing and private Case 2 inputs can materially affect R valuation. No attempt was made to fit assumptions to force agreement with the observed R price.</p>
<h2>4. What buyers can afford</h2>
<p>Maximum whole-loan price per $100 principal, after base servicing and administrative costs, before any investor-level overhead or taxes. These are modeled values, not actual Ally bids.</p>'''
body+=table(['Lifetime net loss','5% return','6% return','7% return','8% return','9% return'],[[f'{v["cnl_pct"]:.0f}%']+[f'{v["asset_price_at_yield"][str(y)]:.2f}' for y in [.05,.06,.07,.08,.09]] for v in [s['loss2'],c,s['loss4'],s['loss6']]])
body+=f'''<p>At a hypothetical price of 107.40, the central unlevered buyer return is {100*c['asset_irr_at_price']['107.4']:.2f}%. At 105 it is {100*c['asset_irr_at_price']['105']:.2f}%. A 7.4% companywide accounting gain is <strong>not</strong> evidence that this pool sold at 107.40. This exercise merely shows why the required return matters as much as borrower APR.</p>
<p>Securitization can yield a different aggregate value because most funding is sold as protected senior securities, while a much smaller residual earns a high required return. The R investor does not pay for the entire loan pool. Do not demand an 18.5% return on every dollar of whole-loan principal or subtract the note coupons again from an unlevered valuation.</p>
<p>Residual value using an 18.5% discount rate, and modeled investor return at the filed $49.272 million R value:</p>'''
labels=[('Central: 3% losses, 1.3% runoff',c),('2% lifetime losses',s['loss2']),('4% lifetime losses',s['loss4']),('6% lifetime losses',s['loss6']),('Faster total runoff: 1.8%',s['fast_attrition']),('Borrower rate lower by 1 percentage point',s['apr_minus1']),('New-issue debt coupons higher by 1 percentage point',s['funding_plus1'])]
body+=table(['Scenario','R value, $m','R return at filed price'],[[label,f"{v['residual_m_at_18_5pct']:.1f}",f"{v['residual_irr_at_filed_value']*100:.1f}%"] for label,v in labels])
body+='''<p>These are prospective scenario returns, not returns earned to date. In the central case, A–D notes receive all modeled cash flows and deliver annual-effective returns of about 3.99–6.37%; N delivers about 5.51%. Minor differences from the pricing sheet’s stated yields reflect day-count and compounding conventions. The residual’s sensitivity explains how bondholders can remain protected while equity-like buyers suffer.</p>
<p>Existing fixed-rate borrower loans and fixed-rate notes do not reprice when market rates change. The borrower-rate and funding-coupon shocks represent a new origination/new issuance comparison, holding other characteristics fixed.</p>
<h2>5. Performance checks and disconfirming evidence</h2>'''
body+=f'''<p>The September collection report shows a $956.172 million ending pool, 86.89% of cutoff principal, 0.06% cumulative net losses and 0.18% greater-than-60-day delinquency. It paid about $1.225 million to R and $0.451 million to XS. These are observed reported distributions, not independently audited borrower receipts or annualized returns. The pool is too young to validate lifetime losses. {link('performance','September servicer report, distributed October 13')}.</p>
<p>Actual runoff is faster than the model’s five-full-month central balance of 88.78%. Closing stubs and collection timing prevent a precise one-number comparison, but this is a reason to retain the faster-runoff downside, not discard it.</p>
<p>Historical final-prospectus Annex I data provide a broader check: 2021-P1 was at 1.53% cumulative net loss after 60 periods; 2022-P3 at 3.46% after 43; 2023-P3 at 3.72% after 32; and 2024-P1 at 1.55% after 25. These are different vintages with different selection and remaining lives. They support a wide loss range, not a claim that 3% is safely conservative. Secondary reproductions of rating opinions cited roughly 2.75–3.0% expectations; the original rating body was inaccessible, so those figures were not treated as independent primary validation.</p>
<p>Checks separate arithmetic from validation: exact cohort-principal reconciliation; an analytical zero-loss/zero-fee loan discounts to par at its contractual yield; monthly principal and waterfall cash conservation; sensible scenario direction; and independent reconstruction of the actual September $2.276 million N principal and $1.225 million residual distributions to cents. The single-average-loan alternative changes the modeled whole-loan price at a 7% hurdle by only about $0.06 per $100 in this scenario. None of these checks validates the assumed lifetime losses.</p>
<h2>6. Servicing and companywide implications</h2>
<p>Raising the base servicing fee from 0.45% to 0.51% reduces central whole-loan value by about $0.12 per $100 at a 7% required return. Raising it to 1% reduces value by about $1.13. Within this trust’s fixed 1% strip, the same increase takes value from XS, leaving R unchanged in the modeled normal waterfall. It would be incorrect to count the fee saving both as higher R value and as XS proceeds. The earlier P3 0.39%/0.45% successor comparison is context, not the fee contract for this P2 deal or for Ally.</p>'''
body+=table(['Channel, nine months through September 2026','Principal sold','What we can infer'],[
 ['Ally','$5.7bn / 41.0%','Contract permits repricing; selection conditional on eligibility. Actual acquisition price, channel gains and cohort return unresolved.'],
 ['Public securitizations','$3.7bn / 26.6%','This P2 case reveals note/R pricing and cash-flow mechanics; other deals may differ.'],
 ['Other fixed pools','$4.5bn / 32.4%','Channel volume known; buyer-specific prices, loan characteristics and returns unresolved.']])
body+=f'''<p>The nine-month accounting bridge remains $14.722bn cash plus $266m new retained value minus approximately $13.904bn implied net basis = $1.084bn gain. This is an accounting consistency check, not an independent fair-price test. Q3’s blended gain ratio is approximately 7.40%, broadly flat against Q2, and cannot be assigned to P2. {link('company','Q3 2026 filing')}.</p>
<p>Every one-point change in sale consideration on Q3’s $5.15bn principal is approximately $51.5m of gain sensitivity, all else equal; on nine-month principal it is $139m. For the securitization channel alone, one point on $3.7bn is $37m. Do not apply this P2 loss or rate shock mechanically to all channels or describe those amounts as forecast losses. Volume, underwriting, capitalized costs, financing penetration and vehicle contribution can offset or amplify the effect.</p>
<h2>7. Competing explanations and decision use</h2>'''
body+=table(['Explanation','Support','What limits it / discriminating evidence'],[
 ['Legitimate origination and securitization value','Publicly priced senior debt and a priced residual can monetize the interest spread. Model supports positive premium.','Need full private Case 2, XS terms and actual settlement/basis bridge; one pool is insufficient.'],
 ['Optimistic but independent buyer assumptions','R returns change sharply with runoff and losses; older bad vintages exceeded 3%.','Current low early losses do not settle lifetime outcome; follow seasoned cohort cash flows and marks.'],
 ['Cheap related-party servicing boosts economics','Low base fee is real; model measures its price impact.','Within fixed strip, value flows to XS. Independent successor contract is partial counterevidence; private channel contracts remain unknown.'],
 ['Unsupported overpayment / subsidy','Would be supported if actual prices require implausible performance or hidden support.','Not established here. Public R yield and independent investor holdings weigh against an inference based solely on a high gain ratio.'],
 ['Accounting or channel mix explains apparent premium','Retained interests and timing differ; company gain is blended.','Needs channel-specific cash, retained value and carrying basis. The withdrawn CarMax comparison supplies no evidence.']])
body+='''<p><strong>Base-case eligible:</strong> observed sale volumes, reported gains, identified contractual terms and filed pricing references. <strong>Sensitivity only:</strong> whole-loan prices, residual returns, servicing-value effects and profit exposures. <strong>Context only:</strong> historical vintage outcomes and investor effective-yield estimates. <strong>Unresolved:</strong> a normalized companywide fair gain ratio, an Ally/private-pool return estimate, or a quantified subsidy adjustment.</p>
<p>The smallest useful next evidence is the P2 certificate Case 2 cash-flow assumptions plus the XS ownership/settlement and the transaction carrying-basis bridge. For the private channels, one dated settlement and the matched purchased cohort are more valuable than additional public APR averages. This pass stops at that boundary: no paid collection, gated-material bypass or invented private pricing.</p>
<p><strong>Investment conclusion:</strong> the evidence is stronger for “Carvana can earn genuine origination/securitization value, but the amount is cyclical and assumption-sensitive” than for either “all elevated gains are suspicious” or “the entire reported gain ratio is validated and sustainable.”</p>
<h2>Reproducibility</h2><p>The accompanying package contains model.py, checks.py, cohorts.json, results.json, central-monthly-cashflows.json, historical-performance.json, checks.json, source provenance and this report. Run model.py, then checks.py. The model consumes the bundled extracted prospectus tables; checks additionally use the existing project loan-level snapshot. Run build_report.py to regenerate this report.</p>'''
style='''body{max-width:1080px;margin:42px auto;padding:0 28px;font:16px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#182936}h1{font-size:32px;line-height:1.2}h2{font-size:22px;margin-top:38px}.date{color:#526578}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:14px;margin:20px 0}th,td{padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #d3dce2;vertical-align:top}th{background:#eaf0f4}a{color:#135b9a}strong{color:#102939}@media print{body{margin:0;font-size:11px}table{font-size:10px}h2{page-break-after:avoid}tr{break-inside:avoid}}'''
(P/'report.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Carvana buyer-return investigation</title><style>'+style+'</style><body>'+body+'</body></html>')
manifest=[]
for file in (P/'sources').glob('*'):
 if file.is_file():manifest.append({'file':str(file.relative_to(P)),'bytes':file.stat().st_size,'sha256':hashlib.sha256(file.read_bytes()).hexdigest()})
(P/'provenance.json').write_text(json.dumps({'as_of':'2026-10-30','links':links,'files':manifest,'source_locator_notes':{'prospectus':'67 hypothetical subpools pp68–70; waterfall pp89–90; reserve ownership p105; Annex I historical prime pools. Hypothetical coupon assumptions in amortization illustration are stale relative to final cover and actual report; model uses final cover/FWP coupons.','pricing':'A–D/N/R pricing table and Case 2 footnotes; expected settlement May27; primary pricing communication, not settlement statement.','performance':'Rows9,104–110, waterfall steps1,14–18; separately verified cash reconciliation.','investor':'Schedule p13; footnote6 effective-yield estimate.'},'search_stopping_rule':'Bounded source families: SEC transaction pricing/contracts/performance; investor filings; ratings; existing peer evidence. Exact Case 2/XS terms not recovered; stop rather than fit to price.'},indent=2))
